  "worked_solution": "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{(2x-3){x}^{3}-3({x}^{2}-3x+3){x}^{2}}{{x}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}-3x-3{x}^{2}+9x-9}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}^{2}+6x-9}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{x}^{4}}\\,\u27f9\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nSo, $x=3$ is the only stationary point of $f(x)$ on $(0,\\infty ).$ Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=1,4$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(1) & =-\\frac{(1-3)^{2}}{{1}^{4}}=-4<0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(4) & =-\\frac{(4-3)^{2}}{{4}^{4}}=-\\frac{1}{256}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ |  | $4$ |\n| ${f}^{\u2032}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $\u2198$ |  | $\u2198$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=3.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=3$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nTherefore, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema on $(0,\\infty ).$",
